med.","rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 66\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>66\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 70\u00b0F, and the record is 83\u00b0F (1889, 1972, 1989). The low of <strong>45\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Savannah is setting record highs far faster than chance","p_rec":"If Savannah's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Savannah has set <strong>53 standing record lows</strong>, close to the 64 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">105 record highs</strong>, far more than the 64 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (58). Savannah's all-time high is 105\u00b0F, on July 12, 1879.","t_chg":"Savannah's nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 151 complete years: Savannah's average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.23 to \u22120.11), about \u22122.5\u00b0F since 1875. Savannah's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.25\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.21 to +0.29), about +3.8\u00b0F since 1875. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.50\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.89) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.48\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.79). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 10 months fell. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Savannah's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.12 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 48.1 inches a year. The wettest year was 1885 (73.94 in); the driest, 1931 (22.00 in). Savannah almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.1 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 9 daily record highs, 4 daily record lows"}}